      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Two things need sorting out first.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.**\n- Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the FDA maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin, and the maximum is 2,000 mg/day for some extended-release forms.\n- A single 1700 mg dose is also well above the usual maximum of about 850 mg to 1,000 mg per dose.\n- Overdosing raises the risk of severe GI effects and lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. A licensed prescriber needs to place or confirm the order.**\nYou've said you're not a clinician. A medication order should come from, or be verified by, the treating prescriber.\n\n**Next steps**\n- Ask the treating prescriber to confirm the intended dose. They may have meant 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day), or 1,700 mg/day split into two doses.\n- Once I have a confirmed dose from the prescriber, I'll enter it right away."
      }
