     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not whether you approve it. You also said you aren't a clinician, so you can't act as the prescriber who verifies it.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, about double the maximum labeled daily dose. That creates a serious risk of harm to the patient, including lactic acidosis.\n\n**What can happen now**\n- **The treating prescriber can review it.** If they intend a dose outside the labeled range, they can enter it themselves and document the reason. That way a licensed clinician is accountable for it.\n- **I can enter a dose within the labeled range** once a prescriber confirms it. Examples are 850 mg three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg twice daily.\n- **Pharmacy can help.** The pharmacy team can help the prescriber work out the right regimen.\n\nIf you give me a prescriber-confirmed dose within the safe range, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
